  "send": {
    "title": "Send your music",
    "blocks": [
      {
        "title": "How it works",
        "text": "Email the artist name, release title, number of tracks, release date and format, with one download link to your mixes, one or two reference tracks and any notes.\n\nPut all files for the release into one folder and zip it. Masters come back as a download, and vinyl masters go straight to your cutting engineer or pressing plant."
      },
      {
        "title": "Preparing your mixes",
        "text": "Stereo WAV or AIFF at 24‑bit or 32‑bit float, at the sample rate of your session. No sample rate conversion, normalising or dither.\n\nPeaks between −10 and −4 dBFS and never above −3 dBFS, set with channel and group faders. No limiters or clippers on the mix bus. A bus compressor that is part of the sound can stay; mention it in your notes.\n\nA second or two of silence at each end, fades printed, files named with track number, artist and title."
      },
      {
        "title": "Stem mastering",
        "text": "Start from a finished mix and split it into six to eight groups, such as kick, bass, drums, percussion, music, effects and vocals, with every effect printed.\n\nAll stems start at the same point and run the same length. Played together, they should sound exactly like your stereo mix, so send that too, one folder per song."
      },
      {
        "title": "Vinyl",
        "text": "De-ess vocals carefully. Sharp esses distort on vinyl and are hard to remove from a finished mix. If you're unsure, send the vocal as a separate stem.\n\nAdd the side split and running order with the time of each side, and book your cutting engineer or pressing plant in advance."
      }
    ],
    "guideButton": "Full guide (PDF)",
    "guideText": "Levels, formats, stems, vinyl and loudness in detail. Print it or pass it on to whoever mixes your music."
  },
